---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Continue from '../components/Continue.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Why international"
  description="Why a Digital Product Passport needs an international core: one laptop sold in five jurisdictions, the current landscape stated honestly, and what neutrality means here."
  eyebrow="02 · Why international"
>
  <h1>Why an international core</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Products cross borders; their passports must not stop at them. One product, one
    identity, one record — with each jurisdiction's rules mounted on it as a profile.
  </p>

  <section aria-labelledby="laptop-heading">
    <h2 id="laptop-heading">A laptop, five jurisdictions</h2>
    <p>
      Consider a laptop assembled in one country from parts made in several more, then sold
      in the European Union, Japan, China, the Republic of Korea, and the United States.
      Each market imposes its own disclosure duties: the European Union requires an ESPR
      product passport; Japan requires energy-efficiency and chemical-disclosure evidence
      under METI schemes; China requires CCC marking and registration on the national
      identity-resolution platform; Korea requires KC conformity; United States federal
      procurement asks for its own evidence.
    </p>
    <p>
      Five duty sets, one physical object. If each duty creates its own passport, the
      product carries five records that can disagree with one another — and the recycler,
      the repairer, and the border officer each meet a different document for the same
      laptop. Divergence is not hypothetical: restricted-substance lists, energy classes,
      and recycling declarations already differ by jurisdiction, and a divergence recorded
      twice can become a contradiction.
    </p>
    <p>
      UniDPP keeps one record — one identity, one append-only event log — and expresses
      each duty as a <strong>profile</strong>: a lens over the same record, owned by its
      regulator, versioned in a registry, carrying its own data points, transforms, and
      trust requirements. Divergent rules stay divergent and visible; the record beneath
      them stays single.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="plate" aria-labelledby="landscape-heading">
    <h2 id="landscape-heading">The landscape, stated honestly</h2>
    <dl class="landscape">
      <div class="land">
        <dt>European standards</dt>
        <dd>
          The EN&nbsp;18216–18246 series, with the ESPR delegated acts, is one regional
          system — the first to be mandated, and explicit in its own text about being
          technology-, ontology-, and domain-neutral. It is treated here as exactly one
          jurisdiction profile among others, on equal footing.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div class="land">
        <dt>National platforms</dt>
        <dd>
          China operates an industrial-identity resolution platform with its own marking
          and cryptographic suites; other states run national code services and
          certificate-body portals. Each serves its jurisdiction well; none is positioned
          as the international layer.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div class="land">
        <dt>Sector systems</dt>
        <dd>
          Battery passports, trade-portability mappings (UNTP), and asset-shell ecosystems
          exist for their sectors. They are integrated as mappings or overlays, never as
          the load-bearing layer.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div class="land">
        <dt>UniDPP</dt>
        <dd>
          A neutral international core under multi-body stewardship: identity, event
          record, semantic registry, and cross-recognition trust — with every profile,
          including the European one, mounted on the same interface. Neutral,
          non-opinionated, and fully open.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <p>
      For a factual, source-referenced comparison of the open implementation efforts, see
      the <a href="/compare/">capability table</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="neutrality-heading">
    <h2 id="neutrality-heading">Neutrality, stated precisely</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Neutral:</strong> the core adopts no jurisdiction's rules. The interface
        that profiles mount on — the lens mount — is what is standardized, because
        proprietary mounts are the lock-in vector.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Non-opinionated:</strong> the core ranks no cryptographic suite, registry,
        or trust framework. The CN profile binds SM suites, the US federal profile binds
        FIPS suites, the EU profile binds eIDAS-acceptable suites; the core carries them
        side by side.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Fully open:</strong> Apache-2.0 / MIT per repository, published conformance
        suites including negative cases, no closed core, no boundary package around a
        private backend.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      What neutrality is not: it is not a claim that jurisdictions agree, and not a promise
      to soften their rules. Divergences are recorded as data — distinct data elements
      harmonized to a common concept — so that a downstream reader can see both rulings and
      the reason for each.
    </p>
  </section>

  <Continue
    href="/framework/"
    num="03"
    title="How it works"
    desc="Six layers, fourteen invariants, and the trust model that lets one record carry many jurisdictions' verdicts across borders."
  />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .landscape {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(15rem, 1fr));
    gap: 1px;
    background: var(--border);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    overflow: hidden;
    margin: 1.4rem 0;
    max-width: none;
  }
  .land {
    background: var(--bg-raised);
    padding: 1rem 1.15rem;
  }
  .land dt {
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    margin: 0 0 0.35rem;
  }
  .land dd {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.86rem;
    color: var(--text-2);
  }
</style>
